## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates the ability to design a small-scale iOS app with proper data persistence, SwiftUI architecture, and accessibility considerations. It tests understanding of Core Data relationships, SwiftUI patterns, data visualization, and inclusive design principles.

## Key Concepts

- Core Data modeling with relationships (Habit + Completion entities)
- SwiftUI MVVM architecture with @FetchRequest
- Data aggregation for heatmap visualization
- Accessibility implementation (VoiceOver, Dynamic Type)
- Core Data migration strategies and unit testing approaches

## Code Example

```swift
// Core Data model sketch
class Habit: NSManagedObject {
  @NSManaged var id: UUID
  @NSManaged var title: String
  @NSManaged var colorHex: String
  @NSManaged var notes: String?
  @NSManaged var photo: Data?
  @NSManaged var completions: Set<Completion>
}

class Completion: NSManagedObject {
  @NSManaged var date: Date
  @NSManaged var habit: Habit
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests CRDT knowledge, offline collaboration, and practical data modeling for cross-device sync. It also evaluates how to bound growth, ensure deterministic reconciliation, and integrate with a common iOS stack.

## Key Concepts
- CRDTs: OR-Set for attachments, observed-remove with vector clocks for changes
- Append-only logs and per-Asset clocks
- Deterministic reconciliation on reconnect
- Realm/SQLite as storage backends
- Lightweight conflict resolver and delta generation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Simple OR-Set merge sketch (JS for illustration)
function mergeORSet(localAdds, remoteAdds, localRemoves, remoteRemoves) {
  const adds = new Set([...localAdds, ...remoteAdds]);
  const removes = new Set([...localRemoves, ...remoteRemoves]);
  return Array.from(adds).filter(x => !removes.has(x));
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test concurrency and network partitions affecting reconciliation?
- How would you prune historical data without breaking causality remediation?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to model simple domain data, perform file-based persistence with Codable, and drive UI with SwiftUI while handling dates and basic filtering.

## Key Concepts
- SwiftUI List and navigation
- Codable JSON persistence to Documents
- File I/O and error handling
- Date arithmetic for overdue
- Lightweight test scaffolding

## Code Example
```swift
struct Task: Codable {
  let id: UUID
  var title: String
  var dueDate: Date
  var isDone: Bool
}

final class TaskStore {
  private var tasks: [Task] = []
  private let url: URL
  init(filename: String = "tasks.json") {
    let dir = FileManager.default.urls(for: .documentDirectory, in: .userDomainMask).first!
    self.url = dir.appendingPathComponent(filename)
    load()
  }
  func load() {
    if let data = try? Data(contentsOf: url), let decoded = try? JSONDecoder().decode([Task].self, from: data) {
      tasks = decoded
    }
  }
  func save() {
    if let data = try? JSONEncoder().encode(tasks) {
      try? data.write(to: url)
    }
  }
  // add/edit/delete helpers...
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you migrate to Core Data if the feature set grows? 
- How would you detect and recover from partial write failures?

## Implementation
- PHPickerViewController (iOS 14+) via UIViewControllerRepresentable for SwiftUI video capture with 10s limit
- AVAssetImageGenerator extracts thumbnails from captured video
- FileManager.documentDirectory stores app-managed .mov and .png files
- SwiftUI LazyVGrid displays searchable grid with TextField search and DatePicker date filter
- @State manages Codable metadata JSON persistence
- All controls need .accessibilityLabel() for VoiceOver

## Key Code
```swift
struct VideoPicker: UIViewControllerRepresentable {
    @Binding var videoURL: URL?
    func makeUIViewController() -> PHPickerViewController {
        var config = PHPickerConfiguration()
        config.filter = .videos
        config.selectionLimit = 1
        let picker = PHPickerViewController(configuration: config)
        return picker
    }
}

struct CheckpointView: View {
    @State private var videos: [VideoMetadata] = []
    @State private var searchText = ""
    @State private var selectedDate = Date()

    var filteredVideos: [VideoMetadata] {
        videos.filter { video in
            video.title.localizedCaseInsensitiveContains(searchText) &&
            Calendar.current.isDate(video.date, inSameDayAs: selectedDate)
        }
    }

    var body: some View {
        LazyVGrid(columns: [GridItem(.adaptive(minimum: 100))]) {
            ForEach(filteredVideos) { video in
                VideoCell(video: video)
                    .accessibilityLabel("Video \(video.title)")
            }
        }
    }
}
```

## Test Plan
1. Record 10s video - verify file saved to Documents
2. Check thumbnail auto-generated
3. Search by title - verify filtering works
4. Filter by date - verify correct results
5. VoiceOver reads all labels correctly

## Why This Is Asked
The prompt tests ability to design a robust, offline-first telemetry system with crash safety, reliable uploads, and pragmatic data modeling.

## Key Concepts
- Offline persistence with Core Data or SQLite for log durability
- Session-scoped aggregation and de-dup payload hashing
- Resilient queue with exponential backoff and idempotent uploads
- Background URLSession transfers and crash-resilience
- Combine-based publishers for live UI updates

## Code Example
```swift
import Foundation
import CoreData

class LogRecord: NSManagedObject {
  @NSManaged var id: UUID
  @NSManaged var sessionId: UUID
  @NSManaged var timestamp: Date
  @NSManaged var level: String
  @NSManaged var message: String
  @NSManaged var payloadHash: String
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test crash-resilience and data integrity across app relaunches?
- How would you handle user privacy when logs contain PII and ensure compliance with app privacy policies?
